  "assumptions": [
    "Idealized small-swing pendulum: period T = 2π√(L/g) with g = 9.81 m/s², so the period is exact in the model and very close for a real pendulum at small angles.",
    "The string is light and doesn't stretch, and the bob counts as a single point at the string's end.",
    "There's no air resistance or friction, so the swing keeps the same 10° amplitude and never dies down.",
    "The swing angle is computed from the timeline's time, so changing the length restarts the swing phase from the current time (the bob may jump).",
    "Large swing angles, where a real period gets a little longer, and damping are not shown."
  ],
  "program": {
    "source": "const g=9.81,A=10*Math.PI/180;\nexport function evaluate({inputs,time}){const L=inputs.length;const T=2*Math.PI*Math.sqrt(L/g);const th=A*Math.cos(2*Math.PI*time/T);return{nodes:{arm:{quaternion:[0,0,Math.sin(th/2),Math.cos(th/2)]},string:{position:[0,-L/2,0],scale:[1,L,1]},bobNode:{position:[0,-L,0]}},readouts:{period:T,lengthOut:L}};}"
  }
